//? Title: Arithmetic Coding: Fractional Bits per Symbol
//? Subtitle: Compressing the handwritten-digit images with datamunge's arithmetic coder under static, adaptive and context models
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Arithmetic coding turns a whole message into one number in \([0,1)\). Each symbol shrinks the
current interval by its probability, so a message of probability \(P\) needs about
\(-\log_2 P\) bits, with no rounding to whole bits per symbol. We derive this bound and then
compress the 115,008 pixel intensities of `dm::datasets::digits()` (values 0 to 16) with
`dm::algorithms`' 32-bit integer coder. With one fixed table of pixel frequencies,
arithmetic coding reaches 2.97667 bits per pixel, which is the entropy to five
decimals. Huffman coding is only 0.7% worse here, because the pixel distribution is not
very skewed. An adaptive model learns the table as it codes. We show that its code length
depends only on the final counts, and that it costs about \(\tfrac{K-1}{2}\log_2 N\) extra
bits. The coder pays off when the model is good. Predicting each pixel from its position
and its left and upper neighbours cuts the cost to 2.23 bits per pixel. In that
model many contexts are almost certain, and Huffman coding, which needs at least one bit per
symbol, would spend 2.37 bits.
)

\toc

> Coding as narrowing an interval

Suppose a source emits symbols \(s\) from an alphabet of size \(K\) with probabilities
\(p_s\), and list the symbols in a fixed order with cumulative probabilities
\(F_s=\sum_{t<s}p_t\). Arithmetic coding starts with the interval \([0,1)\). To code a
symbol, it splits the current interval \([\ell,\ell+w)\) into \(K\) pieces in proportion to
the \(p_s\), and keeps the piece for the symbol that occurred:

$$
\ell \leftarrow \ell + w\,F_s, \qquad w \leftarrow w\,p_s .
$$
\alttext(ell becomes ell plus w times F s, and w becomes w times p s.)

After a message \(s_1,\dots,s_n\) the width is the product of the symbol probabilities, the
probability of the whole message:

$$
w_n=\prod_{i=1}^n p_{s_i}=P(s_1,\dots,s_n).
$$
\alttext(w n equals the product over i from 1 to n of p of s i, which is the probability of the message s 1 to s n.)

Different messages give disjoint intervals, so any number inside the final interval
identifies the message. The encoder sends a short binary fraction \(0.b_1b_2\dots b_L\)
whose whole *dyadic interval* \([0.b_1\dots b_L,\;0.b_1\dots b_L+2^{-L})\) lies inside
\([\ell,\ell+w)\). This also makes the code prefix-free: no longer message can extend the
bits. How long must \(L\) be? The dyadic intervals of width \(2^{-L}\) tile the line. If
\(2^{-L}\le w/2\), at least one of them fits entirely inside an interval of width \(w\).
The smallest such \(L\) is

$$
L=\left\lceil -\log_2 w\right\rceil+1 \;<\; -\log_2 P(s_1,\dots,s_n)+2 .
$$
\alttext(L equals the ceiling of minus log base 2 of w, plus 1, which is less than minus log base 2 of the message probability plus 2.)

\(-\log_2 P\) is the *information content* of the message, and Shannon's source coding
theorem says no uniquely decodable code can beat it on average. So arithmetic coding comes
within two bits of the ideal for the *whole message*, not per symbol. Averaged over the
source, the cost per symbol is the entropy \(H=-\sum_s p_s\log_2 p_s\) plus \(2/n\).
Huffman coding, by contrast, gives every symbol a whole number of bits \(\ell_s\), and its
expected length \(\sum_s p_s\ell_s\) can be up to one bit per symbol above \(H\).

Real numbers of unbounded precision are not practical. The library's `ArithmeticEncoder`
keeps \(\ell\) and \(\ell+w\) as 32-bit integers \(\text{low}\) and \(\text{high}\), and
uses integer counts \(c_s\) with total \(T\) in place of the probabilities. Whenever the
interval falls entirely in the lower or upper half of the register, the leading bit of
every number in it is settled. The encoder then outputs that bit and doubles the interval
(*renormalization*). If the interval straddles the midpoint but sits inside the middle
half, the next bit is not yet known, but it will be followed by its opposite. The encoder
counts these *pending* bits and writes them once the next bit is decided
(*bit-plus-follow*). The decoder repeats the same arithmetic with the bits in view, so both
sides stay in step without carries. Integer rounding of the slices costs a tiny amount
compared with the real-valued coder.

>> Watching the interval shrink

The data are the 1,797 handwritten digits of `dm::datasets::digits()`. Each is an
\(8\times8\) image whose pixels hold ink intensities from 0 to 16. We read every image row
by row into one stream of 115,008 symbols (\(K=17\)), and for now model it with one fixed
table: each intensity's count over the whole stream. The block codes the first image's
first 14 pixels with `dm::algorithms::arithmetic_intervals()`, which tracks the real
interval exactly, and with `arithmetic_bits_trace()`, which records how many bits the
integer encoder has committed after each pixel. The figure follows the first three images.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/arithmetic_coding_trace.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::vector<int> pixels;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < digits.nrows(); ++r)
    for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i) pixels.push_back(static_cast<int>(digits.double_at(std::format("pixel_{}", i), r)));
  const auto counts = alg::symbol_counts(pixels, 17);
  const std::vector<std::uint32_t> freq(counts.begin(), counts.end());
  const double total = static_cast<double>(pixels.size());

  const std::vector<int> head(pixels.begin(), pixels.begin() + 14);
  const auto intervals = alg::arithmetic_intervals(head, freq);
  const auto committed = alg::arithmetic_bits_trace(head, freq);
  std::print("| pixel | intensity | \\(p_s\\) | low \\(\\ell\\) | width \\(w\\) | \\(-\\log_2 w\\) | bits committed |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < head.size(); ++i) {
    const auto [lo, hi] = intervals[i];
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.4f} | {:.15f} | {:.3e} | {:.2f} | {} |\n", i + 1, head[i], counts[head[i]] / total,
               lo, hi - lo, -std::log2(hi - lo), committed[i]);
  }

  const std::vector<int> first(pixels.begin(), pixels.begin() + 192);
  const auto trace = alg::arithmetic_bits_trace(first, freq);
  std::vector<double> x, info, bits;
  double cumulative = 0;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < first.size(); ++i) {
    cumulative -= std::log2(counts[first[i]] / total);
    x.push_back(static_cast<double>(i + 1));
    info.push_back(cumulative);
    bits.push_back(static_cast<double>(trace[i]));
  }
  const auto coded = alg::arithmetic_encode(first, freq);
  std::print("\nAfter 192 pixels: information content {:.1f} bits, bits committed {}, finished code {} bits.\n",
             cumulative, trace.back(), coded.size());

  std::vector<double> lag;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < x.size(); ++i) lag.push_back(bits[i] - info[i]);
  const auto [lo, hi] = std::ranges::minmax(lag);
  std::print("Bits committed minus information content ranges from {:.2f} to {:.2f}.\n", lo, hi);

  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                 .lines(x, bits, "bits committed", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 3.0)
                 .lines(x, info, "information", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.2)
                 .abline_v(64.5, dm::RGB{156, 163, 175}, 1.0)
                 .abline_v(128.5, dm::RGB{156, 163, 175}, 1.0)
                 .legend({"bits committed", "information"}, {dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}})
                 .y_limits(0, 700)
                 .title("Coding the first three images")
                 .x_label("pixels coded")
                 .y_label("bits"));
  layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                 .lines(x, lag, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 1.5)
                 .abline_h(0, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.0)
                 .abline_v(64.5, dm::RGB{156, 163, 175}, 1.0)
                 .abline_v(128.5, dm::RGB{156, 163, 175}, 1.0)
                 .y_limits(-2.5, 0.5)
                 .title("Committed minus information")
                 .x_label("pixels coded")
                 .y_label("bits"));
  layout.size(1200, 440).save_svg("build/arithmetic_coding_trace.svg");
}
```
