Entropy H = 2.9767 bits; Huffman expected length = 2.9967 bits (+0.67%); Kraft sum = 1; fixed-length code = 5 bits.
\image(figures/arithmetic_coding_distribution.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: probability of each pixel intensity over all 115,008 pixels. Right: the ideal code length of each intensity (red) and its Huffman codeword length (blue).)
\alttext(Left: a bar chart of probability against intensity 0 to 16. Intensity 0 has probability 0.49, intensity 16 has 0.09, and every other intensity is between 0.02 and 0.04. Right: points of code length in bits against intensity. The ideal length is 1.03 bits for intensity 0, 3.46 for 16, and between 4.7 and 5.5 for the rest. Huffman lengths are 1 for intensity 0, 4 for 16, 6 for intensities 6 and 9, and 5 for the rest.)

Nearly half of all pixels (49%) are blank and 9% are fully inked. The remaining 15
intensities share the other 42% almost evenly. The entropy is 2.977 bits, well below the 5
bits of a fixed-length code and the 8 bits of the raw bytes. Huffman's integer lengths
track the ideal ones closely. Blank pixels cost 1 bit against an ideal 1.03, and the middle
intensities cost 5 or 6 against ideals between 4.7 and 5.5. The rounding errors partly
cancel, so the expected length is 2.997 bits, only 0.67% above the entropy. This source is
not skewed enough to show off arithmetic coding: no symbol is much more likely than
\(1/2\).

> One fixed table: arithmetic against Huffman

Now code the whole stream. We compare four costs in bits per pixel: a fixed-length code
(5 bits are needed for 17 values), Huffman's expected length, the entropy, and the actual
length of `dm::algorithms::arithmetic_encode()` using the counts as the model. A static
model also has to send its table to the decoder. Seventeen counts of at most 17 bits each
cost under 300 bits, or 0.003 bits per pixel, and we leave them out below.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::vector<int> pixels;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < digits.nrows(); ++r)
    for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i) pixels.push_back(static_cast<int>(digits.double_at(std::format("pixel_{}", i), r)));
  const auto counts = alg::symbol_counts(pixels, 17);
  const std::vector<std::uint32_t> freq(counts.begin(), counts.end());
  const double n = static_cast<double>(pixels.size());

  const auto start = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
  const auto code = alg::arithmetic_encode(pixels, freq);
  const auto mid = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
  const bool round_trip = alg::arithmetic_decode(code, freq, static_cast<int>(pixels.size())) == pixels;
  const auto stop = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();

  const auto lengths = alg::huffman_code_lengths(counts);
  double huffman = 0.0;
  for (int s = 0; s < 17; ++s) huffman += counts[s] * lengths[s];
  const double ideal = alg::ideal_code_length(pixels, freq);

  std::print("| code | total bits | bits per pixel | compressed bytes |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  auto row = [&](std::string name, double bits) {
    std::print("| {} | {:.0f} | {:.5f} | {:.0f} |\n", name, bits, bits / n, std::ceil(bits / 8));
  };
  row("raw bytes", 8 * n);
  row("fixed length (5 bits)", 5 * n);
  row("Huffman", huffman);
  row("ideal \\(-\\log_2 P\\) (entropy)", ideal);
  row("arithmetic coder", static_cast<double>(code.size()));
  std::print("\nArithmetic code minus ideal: {:.2f} bits for the whole stream. Round trip exact: {}. "
             "Encode {:.1f} ms, decode {:.1f} ms.\n",
             code.size() - ideal, round_trip,
             std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(mid - start).count(),
             std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(stop - mid).count());
}
```
