| fixed length (5 bits) | 575040 | 5.00000 | 71880 |
| Huffman | 344640 | 2.99666 | 43080 |
| ideal \(-\log_2 P\) (entropy) | 342341 | 2.97667 | 42793 |
| arithmetic coder | 342341 | 2.97667 | 42793 |

Arithmetic code minus ideal: 0.36 bits for the whole stream. Round trip exact: true. Encode 2.9 ms, decode 3.0 ms.
// result_end

The arithmetic coder lands on the ideal length: 342,341 bits against an ideal of 342,340.6.
The whole stream costs 0.36 bits more than \(-\log_2 P\), which is the two-bit bound with
room to spare, and the 32-bit integer slices add nothing visible. Huffman needs 2,299 bits
more (0.67%). Both cut the 115,008 raw bytes to about 43 kB, a ratio of 2.7. Decoding
recovers every pixel. Each direction takes a few milliseconds on this machine.

> Learning the table as you go

A static model needs two passes over the data and a transmitted table. An *adaptive* model
avoids both. Encoder and decoder start from the same counts, all equal to one, and update
them after every symbol. Before symbol \(i+1\), with \(n_s\) earlier occurrences of each
symbol out of \(i\), symbol \(s\) gets Laplace's rule-of-succession probability

$$
q(s)=\frac{n_s+1}{i+K}.
$$
\alttext(q of s equals n s plus 1, over i plus K.)

>> The code length depends only on the counts

Multiply these probabilities over a message of length \(N\) with final counts \(n_s\).
The denominators run through \(K, K+1, \dots, N+K-1\) whatever the order. The numerators for
symbol \(s\) run through \(1, 2, \dots, n_s\), however its occurrences are spread out. So

$$
Q(s_1,\dots,s_N)=\frac{(K-1)!\,\prod_s n_s!}{(N+K-1)!},
\qquad
-\log_2 Q=\log_2\binom{N+K-1}{K-1}+\log_2\frac{N!}{\prod_s n_s!}.
$$
\alttext(Q of the message equals K minus 1 factorial times the product over s of n s factorial, over N plus K minus 1 factorial. Minus log base 2 of Q equals log base 2 of the binomial coefficient N plus K minus 1 choose K minus 1, plus log base 2 of N factorial over the product of n s factorials.)

This has a clean reading as a two-part code. The first term is the cost of sending the
counts: there are \(\binom{N+K-1}{K-1}\) ways to split \(N\) into \(K\) counts. The second
is the cost of saying which of the \(N!/\prod_s n_s!\) orderings with those counts occurred.
`dm::algorithms::adaptive_code_length()` evaluates it exactly. Stirling's formula,
\(\log n!\approx n\log n-n+\tfrac12\log(2\pi n)\), turns it into the redundancy over the
empirical entropy \(\hat H\) of the message:

$$
-\log_2 Q-N\hat H\approx\frac{K-1}{2}\log_2\frac{N}{2\pi}-\frac12\sum_s\log_2\hat p_s-\log_2 (K-1)!,
\qquad \hat p_s=\frac{n_s}{N}.
$$
\alttext(Minus log base 2 of Q minus N times H hat is approximately K minus 1 over 2 times log base 2 of N over 2 pi, minus one half of the sum over s of log base 2 of p hat s, minus log base 2 of K minus 1 factorial, where p hat s equals n s over N.)

`adaptive_redundancy_approx()` evaluates this approximation. The leading term is the famous
\(\tfrac{K-1}{2}\log_2 N\): each of the \(K-1\) free parameters costs about half a log of
the sample size, the same price as in Rissanen's minimum description length and in BIC. Per
symbol, the cost of learning falls like \(\log N/N\). The block codes growing prefixes of
the pixel stream adaptively and compares the encoder's length with the exact formula and
with the approximation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/arithmetic_coding_adaptive.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::vector<int> pixels;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < digits.nrows(); ++r)
    for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i) pixels.push_back(static_cast<int>(digits.double_at(std::format("pixel_{}", i), r)));

  std::print("| \\(N\\) | \\(\\hat H\\) | \\(N\\hat H\\) | exact \\(-\\log_2 Q\\) | coder bits | "
             "redundancy | Stirling approximation |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> log_n, redundancy, approx_x, approx;
  std::vector<std::size_t> sizes;
  for (std::size_t n = 32; n < pixels.size(); n *= 2) sizes.push_back(n);
  sizes.push_back(pixels.size());
  for (std::size_t n : sizes) {
    const std::vector<int> prefix(pixels.begin(), pixels.begin() + static_cast<std::ptrdiff_t>(n));
    const auto counts = alg::symbol_counts(prefix, 17);
    const double h = alg::shannon_entropy_bits(counts);
    const double exact = alg::adaptive_code_length(counts);
    const auto bits = alg::arithmetic_encode_adaptive(prefix, 17).size();
    const bool all_seen = std::ranges::all_of(counts, [](auto c) { return c > 0; });
    const double r = exact - n * h;
    log_n.push_back(std::log2(static_cast<double>(n)));
    redundancy.push_back(r);
    std::string a = "(some count is 0)";
    if (all_seen) {
      const double ar = alg::adaptive_redundancy_approx(counts);
      approx_x.push_back(std::log2(static_cast<double>(n)));
      approx.push_back(ar);
      a = std::format("{:.1f}", ar);
    }
    std::print("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.1f} | {:.1f} | {} | {:.1f} | {} |\n", n, h, n * h, exact, bits, r, a);
  }
  std::vector<double> lead;
  for (double l : log_n) lead.push_back(8.0 * l);

  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .points(log_n, redundancy, "exact", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 6.0)
      .lines(approx_x, approx, "Stirling approximation", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.5)
      .lines(log_n, lead, "(K-1)/2 log2 N", dm::RGB{156, 163, 175}, 1.5)
      .legend({"exact -log2 Q - N H", "Stirling approximation", "(K-1)/2 log2 N"},
              {dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, dm::RGB{156, 163, 175}})
      .title("Cost of learning the pixel distribution")
      .x_label("log2 N (pixels coded)")
      .y_label("redundancy over N H (bits)")
      .y_limits(0, 160)
      .size(900, 480)
      .save_svg("build/arithmetic_coding_adaptive.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| \(N\) | \(\hat H\) | \(N\hat H\) | exact \(-\log_2 Q\) | coder bits | redundancy | Stirling approximation |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 32 | 2.912 | 93.2 | 115.2 | 116 | 22.0 | (some count is 0) |
| 64 | 3.039 | 194.5 | 220.9 | 222 | 26.5 | (some count is 0) |
| 128 | 2.943 | 376.7 | 409.8 | 411 | 33.2 | 32.3 |
| 256 | 2.977 | 762.2 | 802.7 | 804 | 40.5 | 40.0 |
| 512 | 2.901 | 1485.5 | 1534.1 | 1535 | 48.6 | 48.4 |
| 1024 | 2.982 | 3053.9 | 3109.8 | 3111 | 55.9 | 55.8 |
| 2048 | 2.949 | 6038.8 | 6102.8 | 6104 | 64.0 | 63.9 |
| 4096 | 2.969 | 12160.2 | 12231.9 | 12233 | 71.7 | 71.7 |
| 8192 | 2.912 | 23851.3 | 23931.3 | 23933 | 80.1 | 80.1 |
| 16384 | 2.906 | 47617.6 | 47705.8 | 47707 | 88.2 | 88.2 |
| 32768 | 2.963 | 97105.6 | 97201.4 | 97202 | 95.8 | 95.8 |
| 65536 | 2.987 | 195785.2 | 195888.7 | 195890 | 103.6 | 103.6 |
| 115008 | 2.977 | 342340.6 | 342450.8 | 342452 | 110.1 | 110.1 |
\image(figures/arithmetic_coding_adaptive.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Redundancy of the adaptive Laplace model over the empirical entropy of each prefix (blue points). The red line is the Stirling approximation, drawn once every intensity has occurred. The grey line is the leading term alone.)
\alttext(A scatter plot of redundancy in bits against log2 N from 5 to about 16.8. The exact redundancy rises almost linearly from 22 bits at N equals 32 to 110 bits at N equals 115,008. The Stirling approximation, a red line starting at log2 N equals 7, passes through every point. A grey line for the leading term alone runs parallel to the points but about 18 to 25 bits above them.)

The encoder's length is always within two bits of the exact formula, for example 342,452
bits against \(-\log_2 Q=342{,}450.8\) for the full stream. The order-independence
argument involves no approximation. Once every intensity has occurred, from \(N=128\) on, the
Stirling approximation agrees with the exact redundancy to within 1 bit, and to 0.1 bits
from \(N=1024\). Each doubling of \(N\) adds about 8 bits, which is
\(\tfrac{K-1}{2}=8\) as predicted. The lower-order terms keep the curve about 25 bits
below the leading term. Over the whole stream, learning the table costs 110 bits, or 0.001
bits per pixel, less than the 300 bits a static model would need to send its table.

> Context models: where the fractional bits pay off

An order-0 model, adaptive or not, can never beat the entropy of the pixel distribution,
about 2.98 bits. To do better the model must *predict*, giving each pixel a distribution
that depends on what the decoder has already seen. Let \(C\) be a *context*: any function of
the earlier pixels. If we keep one adaptive table per context, the code length is the sum,
over contexts, of the adaptive length of the pixels coded in that context. Dividing by \(N\)
and letting \(N\) grow, this tends to the *conditional entropy*

$$
H(X\mid C)=-\sum_c P(c)\sum_s P(s\mid c)\log_2 P(s\mid c) \;=\; H(X)-I(X;C)\ \le\ H(X),
$$
\alttext(H of X given C equals minus the sum over c of P of c times the sum over s of P of s given c times log base 2 of P of s given c, which equals H of X minus the mutual information between X and C, and is at most H of X.)

so every context helps, by the mutual information between the context and the pixel. But
each context also pays its own \(\tfrac{K-1}{2}\log_2 n_c\) learning cost, so too many
contexts spread the data too thin (*context dilution*). The decoder must be able to compute
the context, so it may only look at pixels already decoded. `arithmetic_encode_adaptive()`
takes the context as a function of the history, and `context_counts()` returns the counts
in each context. From those we compute the conditional entropy and the predicted length
\(\sum_c\) `adaptive_code_length`\((n_{c,\cdot})\).

The contexts below use what a digit image offers. The *left* and *upper* neighbours are
taken as 0 at the image edges. The *position* is the pixel's index 0 to 63 within its image:
the corners of a centred digit are nearly always blank. *Quantized* neighbours map an
intensity to four levels: 0, 1 to 5, 6 to 11, and 12 to 16.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/arithmetic_coding_contexts.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::vector<int> pixels;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < digits.nrows(); ++r)
    for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i) pixels.push_back(static_cast<int>(digits.double_at(std::format("pixel_{}", i), r)));
  const double n = static_cast<double>(pixels.size());

  // Contexts may look only at the pixels already coded (the history).
  auto left = [](const std::vector<int>& h) { return h.size() % 8 != 0 ? h[h.size() - 1] : 0; };
  auto up = [](const std::vector<int>& h) { return h.size() % 64 >= 8 ? h[h.size() - 8] : 0; };
  auto position = [](const std::vector<int>& h) { return static_cast<int>(h.size() % 64); };
  auto level = [](int v) { return v == 0 ? 0 : v <= 5 ? 1 : v <= 11 ? 2 : 3; };

  struct Model {
    std::string name;
    alg::ArithmeticContextFunction context;
    std::size_t contexts;
  };
  const std::vector<Model> models = {
      {"none (order 0)", {}, 1},
      {"left", left, 17},
      {"up", up, 17},
      {"position", position, 64},
      {"left x up", [&](const auto& h) { return left(h) * 17 + up(h); }, 289},
      {"quantized left x up", [&](const auto& h) { return level(left(h)) * 4 + level(up(h)); }, 16},
      {"position x quantized left", [&](const auto& h) { return position(h) * 4 + level(left(h)); }, 256},
      {"position x quantized left x up",
       [&](const auto& h) { return position(h) * 16 + level(left(h)) * 4 + level(up(h)); }, 1024},
  };

  std::print("| context | contexts | \\(H(X\\mid C)\\) | predicted | coder | learning cost | bytes | "
             "ratio to raw | round trip |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
  std::vector<double> x, bpp;
  std::vector<std::string> labels;
  for (std::size_t m = 0; m < models.size(); ++m) {
    const auto& model = models[m];
    const auto table = alg::context_counts(pixels, 17, model.context, model.contexts);
    double conditional = 0.0, predicted = 0.0;
    for (const auto& row : table) {
      double nc = 0.0;
      for (auto c : row) nc += static_cast<double>(c);
      conditional += nc * alg::shannon_entropy_bits(row);
      predicted += alg::adaptive_code_length(row);
    }
    const auto code = alg::arithmetic_encode_adaptive(pixels, 17, model.context, model.contexts);
    const bool ok = alg::arithmetic_decode_adaptive(code, 17, static_cast<int>(pixels.size()), model.context,
                                                    model.contexts) == pixels;
    const double bits = static_cast<double>(code.size());
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.0f} | {:.2f} | {} |\n", model.name, model.contexts,
               conditional / n, predicted / n, bits / n, (bits - conditional) / n, std::ceil(bits / 8),
               8 * n / bits, ok);
    x.push_back(static_cast<double>(m + 1));
    bpp.push_back(bits / n);
    labels.push_back(model.name);
  }

  std::vector<double> pos;
  for (double v : x) pos.push_back(v);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .bars(x, bpp, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 0.7)
      .abline_h(alg::shannon_entropy_bits(alg::symbol_counts(pixels, 17)), dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.5)
      .x_ticks(pos, {"order 0", "left", "up", "position", "left x up", "q(left) x q(up)", "pos x q(left)",
                     "pos x q(l) x q(u)"})
      .y_limits(0, 3.2)
      .title("Bits per pixel by context model")
      .x_label("context")
      .y_label("bits per pixel")
      .size(1100, 480)
      .save_svg("build/arithmetic_coding_contexts.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| context | contexts | \(H(X\mid C)\) | predicted | coder | learning cost | bytes | ratio to raw | round trip |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
| none (order 0) | 1 | 2.9767 | 2.9776 | 2.9776 | 0.0010 | 42807 | 2.69 | true |
| left | 17 | 2.6982 | 2.7087 | 2.7087 | 0.0105 | 38941 | 2.95 | true |
| up | 17 | 2.5819 | 2.5918 | 2.5918 | 0.0099 | 37260 | 3.09 | true |
| position | 64 | 2.4127 | 2.4559 | 2.4559 | 0.0432 | 35306 | 3.26 | true |
| left x up | 289 | 2.3744 | 2.4625 | 2.4625 | 0.0881 | 35402 | 3.25 | true |
| quantized left x up | 16 | 2.4355 | 2.4458 | 2.4458 | 0.0103 | 35161 | 3.27 | true |
| position x quantized left | 256 | 2.2237 | 2.3286 | 2.3286 | 0.1049 | 33476 | 3.44 | true |
| position x quantized left x up | 1024 | 2.0220 | 2.2342 | 2.2342 | 0.2121 | 32119 | 3.58 | true |
\image(figures/arithmetic_coding_contexts.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Bits per pixel achieved by the adaptive arithmetic coder under each context model. The red line is the order-0 entropy, the best any context-free model can do.)
\alttext(A bar chart of bits per pixel for eight context models: order 0 at 2.98, left 2.71, up 2.59, position 2.46, full left by up 2.46, quantized left by quantized up 2.45, position by quantized left 2.33, and position by quantized left by quantized up 2.23. A red horizontal line at 2.98 marks the order-0 entropy.)

Every context beats the order-0 entropy, and the coder always matches the predicted
length \(\sum_c\) `adaptive_code_length` to the bit. A pixel resembles the one
above it more than the one to its left (2.59 against 2.71 bits). The position alone, at
2.46 bits, is better than either neighbour.

The two left-by-up models show context dilution. Using both neighbours at full resolution
gives 289 contexts and the lowest conditional entropy of the pair, 2.374 bits. But it
pays 0.088 bits per pixel to learn them, and ends at 2.463 bits. Quantizing each neighbour to
four levels keeps only 16 contexts, gives up 0.06 bits of conditional entropy, and pays
just 0.010 bits to learn, so it wins at 2.446. The best model combines the position with
the two quantized neighbours. Its 1,024 contexts have a conditional entropy of only 2.022
bits but cost 0.212 bits per pixel to learn, so it codes at 2.234 bits per pixel. That is 25%
below the order-0 coder, and a compression ratio of 3.58 on the raw bytes. More data per
context would bring the cost closer to the 2.02 bits.

>> Why a whole-bit code cannot keep up

A context model makes many conditional distributions extremely skewed. In the first pixel
of an image, intensity 0 is almost certain. Its information content is a small fraction of
a bit, but a Huffman code must still spend at least one bit on it. To see the size of the
effect, give Huffman the most favourable setting: a separate optimal code for each context,
built from that context's final counts, with the tables sent for free. The figure plots,
for each of the 64 position contexts, the conditional entropy against the Huffman code's
expected length.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/arithmetic_coding_huffman.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  std::vector<int> pixels;
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < digits.nrows(); ++r)
    for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i) pixels.push_back(static_cast<int>(digits.double_at(std::format("pixel_{}", i), r)));
  const double n = static_cast<double>(pixels.size());

  auto left = [](const std::vector<int>& h) { return h.size() % 8 != 0 ? h[h.size() - 1] : 0; };
  auto up = [](const std::vector<int>& h) { return h.size() % 64 >= 8 ? h[h.size() - 8] : 0; };
  auto position = [](const std::vector<int>& h) { return static_cast<int>(h.size() % 64); };
  auto level = [](int v) { return v == 0 ? 0 : v <= 5 ? 1 : v <= 11 ? 2 : 3; };

  // Totals over all contexts: conditional entropy against per-context Huffman codes.
  auto compare = [&](const std::vector<std::vector<std::uint64_t>>& table, std::vector<double>* h_out,
                     std::vector<double>* l_out) {
    double entropy = 0.0, huffman = 0.0;
    for (const auto& row : table) {
      double nc = 0.0;
      for (auto c : row) nc += static_cast<double>(c);
      if (nc == 0.0) continue;
      const auto lengths = alg::huffman_code_lengths(row);
      double bits = 0.0;
      for (std::size_t s = 0; s < row.size(); ++s) bits += static_cast<double>(row[s]) * lengths[s];
      const double h = alg::shannon_entropy_bits(row);
      entropy += nc * h;
      huffman += bits;
      if (h_out) {
        h_out->push_back(h);
        l_out->push_back(bits / nc);
      }
    }
    return std::pair{entropy / n, huffman / n};
  };

  std::vector<double> h, l;
  const auto by_position = compare(alg::context_counts(pixels, 17, position, 64), &h, &l);
  const auto full = compare(alg::context_counts(pixels, 17,
                                                [&](const auto& c) {
                                                  return position(c) * 16 + level(left(c)) * 4 + level(up(c));
                                                },
                                                1024),
                            nullptr, nullptr);
  std::print("| context | \\(H(X\\mid C)\\) | per-context Huffman | Huffman excess |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| position | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:+.1f}% |\n", by_position.first, by_position.second,
             100 * (by_position.second / by_position.first - 1));
  std::print("| position x quantized left x up | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:+.1f}% |\n", full.first, full.second,
             100 * (full.second / full.first - 1));
  std::size_t below_one = 0;
  for (double v : h) below_one += v < 1.0;
  std::print("\n{} of the 64 position contexts have conditional entropy below 1 bit.\n", below_one);

  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .points(h, l, "", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 6.0)
      .abline(0, 1, dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 1.5)
      .abline(1, 1, dm::RGB{156, 163, 175}, 1.5)
      .abline_h(1, dm::RGB{17, 24, 39}, 1.0)
      .x_limits(0, 4)
      .y_limits(0, 5)
      .title("Huffman per position context")
      .x_label("conditional entropy H(X | position) (bits)")
      .y_label("Huffman expected length (bits)")
      .size(800, 560)
      .save_svg("build/arithmetic_coding_huffman.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| context | \(H(X\mid C)\) | per-context Huffman | Huffman excess |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| position | 2.4127 | 2.6862 | +11.3% |
| position x quantized left x up | 2.0220 | 2.3715 | +17.3% |

18 of the 64 position contexts have conditional entropy below 1 bit.
\image(figures/arithmetic_coding_huffman.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Each point is one of the 64 pixel positions. Red: the entropy line \(y=x\) that arithmetic coding follows. Grey: \(y=x+1\), Huffman's worst case. Black: the one-bit floor of any whole-bit code.)
\alttext(A scatter plot of Huffman expected length against conditional entropy for 64 position contexts, both axes in bits. A cluster of points sits at entropy near 0 and length 1, on the black horizontal line at 1 bit. Points between entropy 0.1 and 1 lie just above the line at heights 1.0 to 1.3. Points with entropy above 2 lie close to the red line y equals x, from about 2.2 up to 3.95 bits. All points lie between the red line y equals x and the grey line y equals x plus 1.)

Where a context is uncertain, above about 2 bits, Huffman follows the entropy line almost
exactly, as it did for the whole stream. But 18 of the 64 positions have conditional
entropy below one bit. They are the leftmost and rightmost columns, plus one pixel next to each
in the top and bottom rows: the digits are centred and narrower than the frame, so these
pixels are nearly always blank. There Huffman sits on its one-bit floor while arithmetic coding pays close to the
entropy. Summed over the stream, per-context Huffman needs 11.3% more bits than the
conditional entropy for the position model. For the best model, whose contexts are even
more certain, the excess grows to 17.3%: 2.372 bits per pixel against 2.022. This is
more than the adaptive arithmetic coder actually spends (2.234 bits), even though Huffman
was given each context's true counts and free tables. The better the model, the more of
its gain only an arithmetic coder can turn into fewer bits.

> Summary

- An arithmetic code for a message of probability \(P\) has fewer than
  \(-\log_2 P+2\) bits. On the 115,008 digit pixels the 32-bit integer coder came within
  0.36 bits of the ideal length.
- With a single fixed table, Huffman coding is only 0.67% worse, because no pixel
  intensity has probability much above \(1/2\).
- An adaptive Laplace model costs exactly
  \(\log_2\binom{N+K-1}{K-1}+\log_2\frac{N!}{\prod n_s!}\) bits, whatever the order of
  the symbols. That is about \(\tfrac{K-1}{2}\log_2 N\) bits more than the empirical
  entropy, 110 bits for the whole stream.
- Context models trade information against learning cost. Predicting from the position and
  the quantized left and upper neighbours reaches 2.23 bits per pixel, 25% less than any
  context-free model, while the full-resolution neighbours lose to their quantized versions.
- Good contexts make distributions skewed, and there whole-bit codes waste up to a bit per
  symbol. Per-context Huffman codes, even with free tables, would spend 17% more than the
  conditional entropy.

>> Character and relatives

Arithmetic coding is the entropy coder inside JPEG's optional arithmetic mode, JPEG 2000,
JBIG2 (for bilevel images, with context models much like ours), and H.264/H.265 as CABAC.
*Range coding* is the same idea renormalized a byte at a time. *Asymmetric numeral systems*
(ANS, used in Zstandard and LZFSE) reach the same compression with table lookups, at
Huffman-like speed. Each symbol costs \(O(1)\) arithmetic plus a model lookup. The
adaptive model here searches its counts linearly, \(O(K)\) per symbol; a Fenwick tree makes
that \(O(\log K)\) for large alphabets.
